--- one-digit candidate distribution vs Geom(2) at several depths ---
  depth j=  0: support=[1]  P(d=1)=1.0000  Renyi2=-0.0000 bits  (unconditioned Geom(2) Renyi2 = alpha = 1.5850)
  depth j=  5: support=[1, 2, 3, 4]  P(d=1)=0.7277  Renyi2=0.7872 bits  (unconditioned Geom(2) Renyi2 = alpha = 1.5850)
  depth j= 15: support=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]...  P(d=1)=0.6569  Renyi2=1.0177 bits  (unconditioned Geom(2) Renyi2 = alpha = 1.5850)
  depth j= 25: support=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]...  P(d=1)=0.6057  Renyi2=1.1850 bits  (unconditioned Geom(2) Renyi2 = alpha = 1.5850)
  depth j= 35: support=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]...  P(d=1)=0.4992  Renyi2=1.5265 bits  (unconditioned Geom(2) Renyi2 = alpha = 1.5850)

  READING: this is the EXACT one-digit marginal under UNIFORM sampling from
  W_c(N,s_N) -- a genuinely different object from round2_verification.py's
  geometric-cylinder-measure Doob h-transform (Part C there). If this
  distribution's Renyi-2 entropy tracks alpha=log2(3) closely at interior
  depths but departs near the boundary (j near 0 or near N), that supports
  classifying the -log2(P)/k asymptote as close to the base-digit Renyi-2
  rate away from endpoint effects, with endpoint-driven deviation -- NOT
  proof of a stationary rate, since finite-N/finite-k effects are exactly
  what is being measured here.

